 "notes": "Model: t(i,s) = t0 + (i*LOOP + o[s] + sum_j w[j]*C_j(i,s))/(1+eps), C_j(i,s) = number of HIGH reads at slot j strictly before sample (i,s). LOOP and o[] are quoted at the nominal 16 MHz clock (62.5 ns/cycle) and come out as exact integer cycles: the loop is 40 cycles with inter-sample gaps [4,5,4,4,4,6,5,8] cycles, i.e. the documented [4,4,4,4,4,6,5,6]=37 plus 1 cycle in the R1->R2 gap and 2 cycles in the BS1->R0 end-of-loop gap. w[] is the extra loop time when a sample reads HIGH (the BTJT-taken / NOP-skipped path, whose cost varies with instruction alignment); it is also whole cycles, [0,-1,0,+2,0,0,0,+1]. Without w the sampler's period-2 loop-length alternation is unreproducible and agreement caps near 85-95% in the frame window. eff_high_1/eff_high_0 stayed at the exact PIO wire values, so the STM8 input threshold introduces no measurable high-width distortion beyond a +2 ns common edge shift (a common rise/fall delay is absorbed by t0; 750/375 exactly scores 99.961% / 99.750%). Mean effective loop period over the captures: 2502.49 ns (mean cycle 63.207 ns, eps = -0.01118, spread across captures only +-0.014%). End-of-loop (slot7->slot0) gap = 8 nominal cycles = 505.7 ns after a LOW read at slot 7 and 9 cycles = 568.9 ns after a HIGH read, versus T1L = 498 ns: that is why the inter-bit low of a 1-bit disappears into it. The loop beats against 2 bit periods by only 2.49 ns/loop, but the w[7] term makes the loop length alternate by a whole cycle, so the merged loops come in runs of 1-2 spread over loops ~21-94 rather than one long run (observed 36-39 merged loops per 'ones' capture, simulated 35-38).",
